\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every fixed $\beta >1$ in Gaussian SK, does a starting configuration sampled from the realized Gibbs law still have conditional total-variation distance exceeding 1/4 at time $\exp (n^{1/10000})$, with joint disorder-and-start probability tending to one? Require both the source continuous and discrete update conventions.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every fixed $\beta >1$ in Gaussian SK, does a starting configuration sampled from the realized Gibbs law still have conditional total-variation distance exceeding 1/4 at time $\exp (n^{1/10000})$, with joint disorder-and-start probability tending to one? Require both the source continuous and discrete update conventions.

