\section*{Stretched-exponential mixing barriers for typical low-temperature SK starts}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For each fixed $\beta>1$, typical SK equilibrium configurations held as starts are claimed to remain unmixed until at least $\exp(n^{1/10000})$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Quantitative Parisi and constrained-overlap bounds produce a locked overlap sign around a reference sample. Disorder rotation and narrow-annulus coverage control rare sign-passage paths over the stretched-exponential horizon; spin reversal gives an equilibrium half-space that those paths cannot reach.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For each fixed $\beta>1$, typical SK equilibrium configurations held as starts are claimed to remain unmixed until at least $\exp(n^{1/10000})$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 227, the selected statements include the high-temperature gap for $\beta<1$, cutoff ratios only for $\beta<1/2$, critical lower mixing scales with a subexponential upper bound, and low-temperature barriers. They do not provide the full critical exponent, the full cutoff location, or the newer autocorrelation limits. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{CriticalSK.lean}, \texttt{CriticalSKMixing.lean}, \texttt{SKBarriers.lean}, \texttt{SKHighTemperature.lean}, \texttt{SKRatio.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CriticalSK.covariance\_and\_linear\_tests}, \texttt{OAI.CriticalSK.critical\_mixing\_bounds}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

